-- title:
--   Ld Pasting NCompleteness Of Tail Lower Bound
-- statement:
--   **The nontrivial pasting hypotheses imply the Bernoulli-tail completeness conclusion.**
--
--   Let $S$ be a good successor strategy and let $F$ be an indexed polynomial family. Assume
--
--   $$
--   \gamma\le1,\qquad \zeta\le1,\qquad d\le q,\qquad 0<d,
--   $$
--
--   and assume that $F$ is consistent with points with error $\zeta$, strongly self-consistent with the strategy state with error $\zeta$, and satisfies the slice-boundedness input at error $\zeta$. For an index $k$, also assume
--
--   $$
--   1\le k,\qquad 400md\le k.
--   $$
--
--   If the Bernoulli-tail mass obeys
--
--   $$
--   1-\kappa\left(1+\frac{1}{100m}\right)
--   -\exp\!\left(-\frac{k}{80000m^2}\right)
--   \le T_{\mathrm{tail}}(S,F,k),
--   $$
--
--   then the constructed pasted submeasurement has completeness at least
--
--   $$
--   1-\kappa\left(1+\frac{1}{100m}\right)-\nu_{\mathrm{paste}}
--   -\exp\!\left(-\frac{k}{80000m^2}\right),
--   $$
--
--   with $\nu_{\mathrm{paste}}=100k^2m(\varepsilon^{1/32}+\delta^{1/32}+\gamma^{1/32}+\zeta^{1/32}+(d/q)^{1/32})$. The theorem is the completeness input for the canonical nontrivial pasted measurement.
--
--   Formalization credit: Sirui Lu, Ruixuan Deng, Yanqiao Zhu, and Zhengfeng Ji (arXiv:2609.19814). Mathematical source: Zhengfeng Ji, Anand Natarajan, Thomas Vidick, John Wright, and Henry Yuen (arXiv:2009.12982).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The source declaration is `MIPStarRE.LDT.Pasting.ldPastingNCompleteness_of_tailLowerBound`; its Lean statement gives the exact quantifier order and all boundary hypotheses.
